arned/significance-based KV cache eviction |\n| **TransMLA** (2026) | [arxiv] | Convert GQA models to MLA format for 93-98% KV cache reduction |\n| **DASH** (2025) | [arxiv] | Input-aware dynamic layer skipping as MDP |\n| **DiffSkip** (ACL 2025) | [aclanthology] | Differential FFN layer skipping during generation |\n| **TENET** (2025) | Microsoft Research | LUT-centric architecture for ternary LLM inference on edge |\n\n### 6. NPUs — Limited VRAM Relief Today, Improving Slowly\n\n**Apple MLX / Unified Memory** (the clear winner):\n- NOT an NPU solution — it's a unified memory architecture\n- MLX on M5: 30-60% faster than llama.cpp for most workloads, 3-4x faster prefill\n- Enables running 70B+ 4-bit models because GPU accesses full system RAM (no VRAM bottleneck)\n- Zero-copy approach eliminates CPU↔GPU data transfer overhead\n- **This is the most impactful \"VRAM reduction\" technology for consumers** — it removes the VRAM concept entirely on Mac\n\n**Intel Core Ultra NPU**:\n- Sweet spot is 4B-8B models; 14B+ is a \"stretch target\"\n- NPU optimized for energy efficiency, NOT raw throughput\n- CPU + iGPU hybrid often outperforms NPU-only for LLM inference\n- OpenVINO 2026.x adds EAGLE-3 speculative decoding, FP8, MoE offloading support\n- **Bottom line**: NPUs don't meaningfully reduce VRAM requirements for large models\n\n**Qualcomm Snapdragon X Elite NPU**:\n- Up to 80 TOPS on X2 Elite, but memory bandwidth hasn't scaled proportionally\n- Software ecosystem fragmentation is the biggest limitation — mainstream tools (Ollama, llama.cpp, LM Studio) often bypass the NPU\n- Best for sub-4B or 7B models with specific QNN-compiled formats\n- **Bottom line**: Still a \"tinkering\" phase; NPU remains idle for most LLM workloads\n\n**NPU verdict**: NPUs are NOT reducing the VRAM burden in any meaningful way for serious LLM workloads. They help with small, always-on background AI tasks. Apple's unified memory is the real consumer advantage, but it's an architecture approach, not an NPU approach.\n\n### 7. Realistic Prediction: What Can 16GB VRAM Run in 2028-2030?\n\n**Optimistic but evidence-backed prediction**:\n\n| Capability | 2026 (Now) | 2028-2030 (Predicted) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Dense models** | 14B-27B at Q4 comfortably | 27B-35B at Q4 comfortably (better quantization, MLA) |\n| **MoE models** | Limited (total params must fit) | 60B-100B total params via better sparse loading & offloading |\n| **Reasoning quality** | R1-Distill-14B level | Significantly better due to continued distillation improvements |\n| **Context window** | ~8K-16K practical | 32K-64K+ practical (KV cache compression: MLA, TurboQuant) |\n| **Multimodal** | Basic vision | Native vision + early audio/video |\n| **Agentic workflows** | Single model | Multiple specialized small models in parallel |\n\n**Key drivers for improvement**:\n1. Continued distillation improvements (frontier knowledge → small models)\n2. KV cache compression (MLA, INT4 KV, TurboQuant) enabling longer contexts\n3. MoE with better sparse loading (only loading active experts)\n4. Dynamic quantization that adjusts precision per-layer\n5. Speculative decoding / MTP for speed without extra VRAM\n\n**Key limiters (counter-evidence)**:\n1. Context windows keep growing — KV cache scales linearly and can exceed model weight size\n2. Reasoning models (chain-of-thought) generate many \"thinking tokens,\" inflating memory use\n3. MoE models require ALL parameters in memory, not just active ones\n4. Multimodal (vision, audio) adds significant VRAM overhead for buffers\n5. The performance cliff when hitting VRAM limits is steep — partial CPU offloading is 5-10x slower\n\n---\n\n## Evidence Table\n\n| Source | Key Claim | Quote/Data | Tier |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Search synthesis (multiple technical sources) | 4-bit quantization is the sweet spot | \"~75% VRAM savings vs FP16 with 1-5% quality loss; AWQ achieves 95%+ of FP16 quality\" | A |\n| arxiv.org (BitNet b1.58 2B4T) | 1-bit models match FP16 at same size | \"2.37x to 6.17x speedup on x86 CPUs; 71.9-82.2% energy savings; 700MB for 2B model\" | A |\n| Microsoft Research (Sparse-BitNet, Mar 2026) | Ternary + sparsity combined | \"1.30x speedup in both training and inference via N:M sparsification\" | A |\n| DeepSeek R1 distillation benchmarks | Small models capture frontier reasoning | \"R1-Distill-14B: 93.9% on MATH-500, fits ~16GB VRAM\" | A |\n| NVIDIA (Nemotron-51B) | NAS compresses 70B→51B effectively | \"2.2x faster inference, 98%+ accuracy retained, fits single H100\" | A |\n| ICLR 2026 (TurboQuant) | KV cache compression breakthrough | \"~5x KV cache compression with near-zero quality loss\" | A |\n| DeepSeek architecture docs | MLA reduces KV cache dramatically | \"93-98% KV cache reduction vs standard MHA\" | A |\n| ICML 2026 (\"Don't Drop Dropout\") | Layer dropout enables elastic inference | \"Up to 1.5x inference speedup with negligible accuracy loss\" | A |\n| Qwen3/Llama 4 model cards | MoE total params still need full VRAM | \"Llama 4 Scout 109B needs ~55GB at 4-bit; Llama 4 Maverick 400B needs ~200GB\" | A |\n| Multiple industry analyses | NPUs don't meaningfully reduce VRAM for LLMs | \"Intel NPU sweet spot is 4B-8B models; Qualcomm NPU best for sub-4B to 7B\" | B |\n| Apple MLX benchmarks 2026 | Unified memory eliminates VRAM bottleneck | \"30-60% faster than llama.cpp; can run 70B+ at Q4 using full system RAM\" | B |\n| Counter-evidence analysis | VRAM requirements still growing for frontier use | \"KV cache can exceed model weight size at long contexts; reasoning models inflate token count\" | B |\n\n## Counter-Evidence\n\n1. **MoE doesn't save local VRAM**: Despite MoE models only activating a fraction of parameters per token, ALL parameters must be loaded into memory. A 400B MoE model still needs ~200GB at 4-bit, even if only 17B parameters are active.\n\n2. **Context windows are growing faster than compression**: The KV cache grows linearly with context length. With models advertising 1M+ token contexts, the KV cache can consume 70-90% of available VRAM, dwarfing the model weights.\n\n3. **Reasoning models are more VRAM-hungry, not less**: Chain-of-thought / \"thinking\" models (DeepSeek-R1, o1/o3-class) generate many internal tokens before answering, significantly inflating per-request memory usage.\n\n4. **BitNet requires training from scratch**: You cannot simply quantize an existing model to 1-bit post-hoc. This limits adoption to models specifically designed with ternary weights from the start.\n\n5. **The \"performance cliff\"**: When a model exceeds VRAM and spills to system RAM, performance drops 5-10x. Efficiency improvements help you stay above the cliff, but they don't eliminate it.\n\n6. **Multimodal adds overhead**: Vision, audio, and video capabilities require significant additional memory buffers beyond the text model weights.\n\n## Gaps\n\n1. **No reliable benchmarks exist for 2028-2030 model capabilities** — predictions are extrapolations from current trajectories.\n2. **BitNet scaling beyond 2B** is still unproven in production. The 100B CPU demo is theoretical throughput, not a deployed consumer product.\n3. **MoE partial expert loading** (only loading active experts into VRAM) is theoretically possible but no production-ready implementation exists for consumer hardware as of September 2026.\n4. **Quantization-aware training (QAT)** adoption rate among open-source model providers is unclear — most models are still post-training quantized by the community.\n5. **LPDDR6 memory bandwidth impact** on unified memory architectures for AI inference lacks published benchmarks.\n\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
