{"step_index":37,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T12:59:11Z","content":"Priyanshu, **no — you cannot upgrade RAM on a MacBook Pro. Period.** And this applies to most modern laptops in 2026, not just Apple.\n\n### Why It's Not Upgradable\n\n**MacBook Pro (any M-series):** RAM is **soldered directly onto the Apple Silicon chip package**. It's part of the SoC die — unified memory is physically bonded to the processor. No slot, no module, no upgrade path. What you buy is what you're stuck with for the laptop's entire life.\n\n**Most Windows \"Pro\" / thin-and-light laptops (Dell XPS, HP Spectre, Lenovo Yoga, ASUS Zenbook):** RAM is **soldered to the motherboard** (LPDDR5/LPDDR5X). Same situation — no upgrade possible.\n\n### Which Laptops Still Allow RAM Upgrades?\n\nOnly **thicker, workstation-class or gaming laptops** with SO-DIMM slots:\n\n| Upgradable | Not Upgradable |\n|---|---|\n| Lenovo ThinkPad P-series (some) | All MacBook Pro / Air |\n| Some Lenovo Legion gaming laptops | Dell XPS 13/14/15/16 |\n| Some ASUS TUF / ROG (check specific model) | HP Spectre / Envy |\n| Framework Laptop 16 (designed for upgrades) | Most ultrabooks under 18mm thick |\n| Some MSI gaming laptops | Surface Laptop / Pro |\n\nEven among gaming laptops, the trend is moving toward soldered RAM. You need to check the **specific model's teardown** before buying.\n\n### What This Means For Your Decision\n\nThis is exactly why the VRAM/RAM decision matters at purchase time:\n\n- **If you buy a MacBook Pro with 16 GB unified memory, that's 16 GB forever.** You'll run 7-8B models. That's it. No upgrade to 32 GB later.\n- **If you buy 32 GB, that's also forever** — but you'll run 14-20B models comfortably.\n- **If you buy a Windows laptop with an RTX 5080 (16 GB VRAM), the VRAM is also non-upgradable** — it's soldered on the GPU die.\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> **On a Mac, system RAM = GPU memory.** So 16 GB unified memory means both your OS, apps, AND AI models share that 16 GB. After macOS and apps take 4-6 GB, you have ~10-12 GB left for models. That's tight even for a 7B model with context.\n>\n> On a Windows laptop, VRAM is separate from system RAM — a 16 GB VRAM GPU has the full 16 GB dedicated to models.\n\n**Bottom line:** Configure the RAM/memory at purchase for what you'll need in 5 years. There is no \"buy cheap now, upgrade later\" option on any modern MacBook or most modern laptops.","thinking":"The user is asking about RAM upgradability on modern laptops, specifically MacBook Pro and other \"Pro\" series laptops. This is a straightforward factual question — no plan needed, no research needed. I know this well.\n\nThe answer is: No, you cannot upgrade RAM on modern MacBook Pros or most modern thin-and-light laptops. Let me be direct and honest about this, applying anti-sycophancy (no sugarcoating)."}
