{"step_index":39,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:01:09Z","content":"Priyanshu, **it depends on which platform — and this is the single most confusing thing about laptop AI, so let me make it dead simple:**\n\n### Mac (Apple Silicon) — YES, RAM = VRAM\n\n```\n32 GB Unified Memory = shared between CPU and GPU\n├── macOS + apps use ~4-6 GB\n└── Remaining ~26-28 GB available for AI models (GPU access)\n```\n\nApple Silicon has **no separate VRAM**. The GPU directly accesses system RAM. So a 32 GB MacBook Pro gives the GPU access to all 32 GB (minus what the OS uses).\n\n---\n\n### Windows Laptop (NVIDIA GPU) — NO, RAM ≠ VRAM\n\n```\n32 GB System RAM ──── for CPU, OS, apps\n16 GB VRAM (separate) ── for GPU, AI models ← THIS is what matters for AI\n```\n\nThese are **two completely separate memory pools** on different chips. A Windows laptop with 32 GB RAM + RTX 5080 has **16 GB VRAM** — not 32 GB. The system RAM number is irrelevant for AI model loading.\n\n---\n\n### The Cheat Sheet\n\n| Laptop | RAM Listed | Actual VRAM for AI |\n|---|---|---|\n| MacBook Pro M5 Pro — 32 GB | 32 GB unified | **~26-28 GB usable** for models |\n| MacBook Pro M5 Pro — 48 GB | 48 GB unified | **~42-44 GB usable** for models |\n| Windows + RTX 5070 + 32 GB RAM | 32 GB system + 12 GB VRAM | **12 GB** for models |\n| Windows + RTX 5080 + 32 GB RAM | 32 GB system + 16 GB VRAM | **16 GB** for models |\n| Windows + RTX 5090 + 32 GB RAM | 32 GB system + 24 GB VRAM | **24 GB** for models |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **When shopping for a Windows laptop, ignore the \"32 GB RAM\" number for AI purposes. Look at the GPU's VRAM number only.** A \"32 GB RAM + 8 GB VRAM\" laptop is worse for AI than a \"16 GB RAM + 16 GB VRAM\" laptop.\n>\n> When shopping for a Mac, the RAM number IS the VRAM number — they're the same pool.\n\nThat's the entire difference. Mac = shared pool. Windows = two separate pools, and only the GPU's pool matters for AI.","thinking":"The user is confused about the difference between RAM and VRAM. This is a fundamental question that needs a clear, simple answer. Let me explain the difference and how it works on different platforms."}
